  "Number": 4,
  "Question": "If \\(M\\) is \\(30\\%\\) of \\(Q,\\) \\(Q\\) is \\(20\\%\\) of \\(P,\\) and \\(N\\) is \\(50\\%\\) of \\(P,\\) then \\(\\frac{M}{N}=\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\frac3{250}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\frac3{25}\\)",
    "C": "\\(1\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\frac65\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\frac43\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "Expressing both \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) as fractions of \\(P\\) lets us compare them directly. The given percentages give\n\\[\nQ=\\frac15P,\\qquad M=\\frac3{10}Q=\\frac3{50}P,\\qquad N=\\frac12P.\n\\]\nThe ratio \\(M/N\\) requires \\(N\\ne0\\), so \\(P\\ne0\\). We may therefore cancel the common factor \\(P\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{M}{N}=\\frac{3/50}{1/2}=\\frac3{50}\\cdot2=\\frac3{25}.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{\\frac3{25}}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{B}}\\).",
